speechtext:
The report of the district engineer is as follows:
The work was of good materials and workmanship;
The contractor omitted bolting the vertical posts to face timbers below the ballast floor, as called for. The .presence of the bolting, as specified, would probably have enabled the work to have stood until completed when it -ivould

Supply-Harbours and Rivers, N.B.
have been safe. Work built in 1926-27 did not fail although the bolts below ballast floor were not required;
The contractor has no legal claim, as the work was not built as specified, the bolts being omitted;
In addition I have to say that the work is one exposed to risk of damage, and although the damaging storm was not unduly severe, the cause to which the damage is attributed is unusual. The floor of the bay at the site is flat rock, with no overburden. The crib was ballasted with as much stone to give it weight as circumstances allowed. The storm came on at a critical time, when the rise and fall of the water level due to wave action was sufficient to produce a lifting and dropping action on the ballasted crib.
The crib was pounded on the rock bottom, the drift bolts loosened so that the crib timbers gradually parted enough to allow the ballast to be released, and the crib, deprived of its supporting load of ballast, was wrecked.
Had the storm held off another twenty-four hours the work would probably have been safe.
